Difftest findings (2026-10-03, agent odin-porter)
================================================
Base commit: c9cf43d4 (Wave 1 closeout + lane board (wave 2))
Toolchain: g++ 16.2.1, odin dev-2026-09:a2fb372b7, python 3.14.7
C++ locale for ranked_match: en_US.utf8 (harness pins LC_ALL, stderr-verified)
`odin run` vs built-binary equivalence: verified for all 4 modules (--smoke-odin-run)

Modules covered: 4 (hash, diff, ranked_match, json)
Total vectors compared: 277,954 across 3 full seeds (20261003, 777, 424242)
  + 1 ranked_match-only seed + pilot (500/module)
Mismatches found: 0. No repros: there is nothing to reproduce.

Coverage notes (seed 20261003 census):
- hash: lengths biased 0..9 (every murmur tail path len&3 + empty), full
  random bytes incl. >=0x80 (fnv1a signed-char quirk), u64 edges for
  combine/hash_values (0, 2^32+-1, 2^63, 2^64-1, ...).
- diff: mutation pairs, 2-letter-alphabet alignments, shared prefix/suffix
  middles, 100..400-byte inputs exercising the cost-limit=1000 fallback.
- ranked_match: 8,145/20,053 vectors contain bytes >=0x80 (valid + invalid
  UTF-8) across match/matchL/cmp/letters; 1,476 cmp vectors exercised a
  real (non-NA) pairwise ordering incl. the UnitTest preferred() pairs.
- json: every C++ outcome class hit and mapped (OK 10197, NULL 1790,
  unable-to-parse-json 3669, is-not-a-number 339, bare [/[ 338/75,
  bad_value_cast 52, expected-comma-or-close 67/35, max-depth 606,
  expected-colon 75); pure-nesting depths 95..108 at ~60 vectors each,
  straddling the 100 boundary; 32-bit overflow numbers; dup object keys.

Verdicts:
- hash: PASS. Bit-identical incl. the sign-extension quirk and wraps.
- diff: PASS. Identical edit scripts incl. cost-limit fallback path.
- ranked_match: PASS under en_US.utf8. No libc-wide-char vs core:unicode
  divergence observed on ~8k non-ASCII vectors per seed. (C-locale
  behavior was not compared; the port documents the tables as its
  deviation there, and Kakoune runs under UTF-8 locales in practice.)
- json: PASS modulo the documented error-model mapping (null/throws -> enum),
  which held for every failure observed: no unmapped C++ error occurred.

Harness bugs found and fixed during bring-up (not port bugs):
- a diff seed vector with 4 TAB fields for a 3-field op (both sides
  correctly reported HARNESS-ERROR);
- the driver decoded harness stdout as strict ASCII while json output
  legitimately contains raw >=0x80 bytes (to_json only escapes \, \",
  <=0x1F); now decoded with the injective backslashreplace transform.

Difftest findings, wave 2 (2026-10-03, agent wave3-difftest2)
============================================================
Base commit: da31bb74 (Wave 2 closeout + lane board (wave 3))
Toolchain: g++ 16.2.1, odin dev-2026-09:a2fb372b7, python 3.14.7
C++ locale for ranked_match/format/utf8: en_US.utf8 (harnesses pin
LC_ALL, stderr-verified)
`odin run` vs built-binary equivalence: verified for all 7 modules
(--smoke-odin-run)

Modules covered: 7 (round-1 four + format, ranges, utf8)
New seeds: 89 format + 97 ranges + 143 utf8 (ranges split seeds mined
from the C++ UnitTest in src/ranges.cc; format/utf8 have no C++
UnitTests, so seeds are hand-built edge cases).
Full run (seed 20261003, count 20000): 145,523 vectors, 131
mismatches, all in utf8 and all one root cause (see below).

Coverage notes (seed 20261003 census):
- format: 20089 vectors; int/uint/hex over edge + random 64-bit,
  grouped over [0, 10^18), float over full u32 bits (2996 vectors),
  cp over [0, INT32_MAX], format/format_to over placeholder soup
  (implicit/explicit/negative/huge-wrapping indices, widths incl.
  zero-pad/negative/empty/invalid, escapes, NUL bytes) with 0-3
  params each. Outcome census: 19291 exact agreements, 586 float
  semantic accepts (161 inf + 178 nan spellings, 247 draws of 3
  known finite tie-break patterns, 0 new), 212 format_to
  precedence accepts, 0 bad.
- ranges: 20097 vectors; every op hit 500+ times (split family 6034
  incl. sep==escaper; skip/drop capped at len; accumulate sums and
  tiny products; for_n_best over distinct ints; static_gather N=1..4
  exact/inexact over non-empty lists).
- utf8: 20143 vectors; byte soup (valid + invalid UTF-8) over
  read/cp/next/finish/previous/advance/distance/prevcp,
  size_cp/dump over [0, INT32_MAX], width over the agreement pool +
  ASCII + negatives + verified huge values, coldist/advcol over
  pool-built strings. 131/20143 mismatch, every one charstart@len
  (proven exhaustive by re-checking all outputs, not just the 10
  shown).

Verdicts:
- format: PASS modulo two documented comparison adjustments (float
  semantic compare; format_to either-true-error rule). Every C++
  error class observed and mapped; no unmapped error occurred.
- ranges: PASS. Bit-identical across all 19 ops incl. the split
  escaper edge cases and for_n_best visit orders.
- utf8: MISMATCH (1 systematic single-point deviation).
  character_start(s, len(s)) on non-empty input: C++ returns len
  (the loop reads *end(), NUL for std::string, so it stops at once),
  Odin returns the last character's start. Repro:
    printf 'charstart\\ta\\xc3\\xa9\\xe2\\x82\\xac\\t6\\n' | bin/utf8_cc   # 6
    printf 'charstart\\ta\\xc3\\xa9\\xe2\\x82\\xac\\t6\\n' | bin/utf8_odin # 3
  (Pass a literal TAB.) Note for the fixer: the C++ value depends on
  the byte after end(), which is only NUL for basic_string-like
  ranges — for a StringView it is an out-of-bounds read. All other
  utf8 vectors (20012/20143) agree exactly.

Exhaustive side probes (throwaway binaries, not committed):
- float: 60018 f32 bit patterns (edges, subnormals, nan/inf, random)
  through to_chars-general vs format_to_string_float. All finite
  values agree except 3 last-digit ties (4f2b0cd1, 51907a15,
  4e3bae0b), both spellings verified to round-trip; inf/nan differ
  only in styling (inf/-inf/nan/-nan vs Inf/-Inf/NaN).
- width: every codepoint 0..0x10FFFF through wcwidth-mapped C++
  vs unicode_codepoint_width under en_US.utf8. 66015 differ (5.9%):
  65377 wide-block-unassigned (1 vs 2), 440 + 1 zero-width/format
  (0 vs 1), 196 emoji/pictographs (2 vs 1), 1 each (0 vs 2, 1 vs 0).
  91 contiguous ranges; see README for the shape. Negatives and
  spot-checked values above 0x10FFFF agree (1). The fuzz
  agreement pool (27 codepoints, widths {0,1,2}) was verified
  point-by-point against this probe.

Harness/driver bugs found and fixed during bring-up (not port bugs):
- format C++ printed raw e.what(), which embeds the fmt's
  index/width field: a newline there broke the line protocol (extra
  output line). Now escaped; the error mapping still applies.
- a ranges seed (static_gather, empty input, N=1) segfaulted the C++
  side: static_gather dereferences end() without checking on empty
  ranges. Empty static_gather is excluded from fuzzing (documented
  C++ UB, found by crashing).
- grouped fuzz bound corrected to < 10^18 (19+ digits overrun the
  C++ buffer; 16-18 digits fit exactly).
- fuzz.py mixed salted hash() into per-module stream offsets, so
  equal --seed runs generated different streams despite the README
  claim. Replaced with a stable string offset; reproducibility
  verified (two runs, identical mismatch counts).

Difftest findings, wave 5 round 3 (2026-10-03, coordinator triage)
===============================================================
Base: wave 5a closeout (651 tests). Toolchain: g++ 16.2.1,
odin dev-2026-09, python 3.14.7. C++ locale for regex/regex_vm:
en_US.utf8 (harnesses pin LC_ALL, stderr-verified).

Modules covered: 5 new (regex, regex_vm, faces, keymap_manager,
parameters_parser) + re-runs of ranked_match, utf8.
New seeds: 174 regex + 100 regex_vm + 100 faces + 36
keymap_manager + 34 parameters_parser (UnitTest-mined + hand-built
edges, incl. invalid-UTF8 pattern/subject soup for regex).
Full runs (count 20000, seeds 20261003 and 777): 5x ~20k vectors,
0 mismatches after the fixes below. faces/keymap_manager/
parameters_parser agreed from the first run; every regex-family
mismatch traced to a real port bug, all fixed with regression tests.

Bugs found and fixed (all verified by re-fuzz to zero + `odin test`):
- face_registry_remove heap corruption: the entry value was freed
  before delete_key, leaving a ghost that a later lookup returned
  (use-after-free). Fix: delete the key first, then free.
  Regression: face_registry_test_remove_repeated_no_ghost.
- utf8 character_start(s, len(s)) (round-2 known mismatch): C++
  stops at once (reads NUL past end()); the port walked back.
  Fix: stop at end of input. Regression: utf8_test_character_start.
  utf8 now fully PASS (20143/20143, was 131 mismatches).
- face_registry word-char validation read names as signed bytes,
  accepting high-byte garbage the C++ rejects. Fix: byte-wise
  compare. Regression: face_registry_test_high_byte_names_rejected.
- regex parser rejected invalid-UTF8 patterns the C++ accepts.
  Root cause: C++ read_codepoint (src/utf8.hh:86) does NOT forward
  its InvalidPolicy to read_codepoint_multibyte, so only
  end-of-input dereferences throw; orphan bytes and truncated
  sequences decode blindly (sign-extended negatives). The parser
  advances byte-wise (to_next) over those values. Fix: decode
  blindly in regex_vm_parser_decode; error only at end of input.
  Related: the C++ stores literals in a 24-bit field, so an orphan
  literal (0xFFFFFFxx) truncates to 0xFFFFxx and can never match
  (proven: `match \x8d \x8d` -> NO); the port truncates likewise,
  and Desc/class-range limits compare unsigned like char32_t.
  Regression: regex_test_invalid_utf8_lenient.
- (?i) folding of orphan bytes: C++ towlower passes invalid values
  through unchanged, while the port wrapped negatives into the
  ASCII table. Fix: unicode_to_lower/to_upper compare the 128 limit
  unsigned so negatives take the wide path (core:unicode passes
  them through bit-identical, probed). The is_word/iswdigit
  predicates keep the signed compare deliberately: negatives fail
  the ASCII branch, matching iswalnum/iswdigit(invalid) == 0.
- iswalnum/iswdigit vs core:unicode (all remaining ctype + \w\W\D
  search mismatches): exhaustive probing of glibc en_US.utf8 over
  U+0000..U+10FFFF against core:unicode queries showed iswdigit is
  exactly ASCII 0-9 (0 non-ASCII members) and iswalnum is letters +
  decimal digits + letter numbers (Nl) + letter-like marks, while
  generic diacritics and other numbers (No: superscripts, vulgar
  fractions) are excluded. Fix: regex_vm_is_digit is ASCII-only;
  new unicode_is_alnum (letters + Nd + a 1586-codepoint / 263-range
  add-table for Nl and letter-like marks, binary-searched) agrees
  with glibc on all 1,114,112 codepoints, 0 diffs. unicode_is_word
  and ranked_match_is_alnum share it (ranked_match re-fuzzed clean:
  20053/20053; one stale test asserting is_word('²') was corrected
  to the C++-observed false, with new edge cases).

No accepts this round: every mismatch was a port bug with a fix.

